{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>When you acquire a Hebrew slave.</b> The Torah intended to begin the first law with the Hebrew slave because it contains a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt and a remembrance of the work of Creation — a remembrance of the Exodus from Egypt, for Israel were slaves to them and the Holy One, blessed be He, brought them out from among them to be slaves to Him alone; and thus it is written (Leviticus 25:55): ״כי לי בני ישראל עבדים עבדי הם אשר הוצאתי אותם מארץ מצרים״. And He mentioned among the laws the law of the Hebrew slave first, just as He mentioned among the Decalogue ״אשר הוצאתיך מארץ מצרים מבית עבדים״ as the first utterance. A remembrance of the work of Creation, for just as the Sabbath is a remembrance of the work of Creation — that the Blessed Name created His world in six days and the seventh day is a day of rest — so too this law is a remembrance of the work of Creation, for the seventh year for the slave is the time of rest from the master's labor, like the seventh day, for the sevenths are chosen forever: in days, and in years, and in the septennial cycles. In days — the seventh day, which is the Sabbath; in years — the seventh year, which is the sabbatical year (shemittah), also called Sabbath; in the septennial cycles — the seventh shemittah, which is the commandment of Jubilee. And all this is one principle flowing from one source, namely the mystery of the days of the world as mentioned in the portion of Genesis through ״ויכלו״." "<b>Six years he shall labor.</b> The verse speaks of a slave whom the court sold for his theft, as it is said (Exodus 22:2): ״אם אין לו ונמכר בגנבתו״. And it states יעבוד — not backbreaking labor and not degrading labor, for the Torah likened him to a hired laborer, as it is said (Leviticus 25:40): ״כשכיר כתושב יהיה עמך״ — just as a hired laborer is in his trade, so too this one is in his trade; and just as a hired laborer works by day and not by night, so too this one by day and not by night." "<b>And in the seventh.</b> The seventh year of his sale, as it is stated: ״שש שנים יעבד״ — and not the seventh year of shemittah. <b>To freedom.</b> This teaches that he does not require a bill of emancipation." "<b>Without charge.</b> That if the slave fell ill while in his domain, he cannot say to him, 'Give me money against the wages of your idleness' or the cost of food and the physician's fee — therefore it is written חנם, free, without money, provided only that he did not fall ill for more than three years, for then he is obligated to make up the time, since the Torah likened him to a hired laborer, and it is written (Isaiah 16:14): ״בשלש שנים כשני שכיר״, and it is written (Deuteronomy 15:18): ״כי משנה שכר שכיר״ and so forth."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:02:04.763939757Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}